// AMPERE SERVES ITS DATASHEETS FROM MORE THAN ONE SURFACE, AND THEY DISAGREE. This block was first
// written on 2026-09-02 from the family product brief alone, which links Altra v1.50 and Altra Max
// v1.25, and it concluded that v1.50 was current. That conclusion was WRONG, and the error is worth
// naming because the measurement itself was sound: the brief was fetched, its links read, and each
// PDF confirmed to resolve. What failed was reading ONE publication surface as the publisher's
// current serving state. The brief is a stale index. Ampere's customer-connect device-documentation
// surface serves newer revisions of both documents.
//
// SO THE INDEX IS PART OF THE FACT, NOT A CONVENIENCE. A supersession standing is relative to what
// the publisher serves NOW, so naming which surface was consulted is what makes the standing
// falsifiable; collapsing two disagreeing pages into "the Ampere page" would conceal exactly the
// stale-index condition this vocabulary exists to expose.
data ampere_altra_device_documentation_authority: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "amperecomputing.com/customer-connect/products/altra#device-documentation"
  }
}

// THE NON-MAX ALTRA CITATION IS SUPERSEDED BY ISSUE 1.55, VERIFIED BY RETRIEVAL. The locator below
// was fetched on 2026-09-02: HTTP 200, 6630790 bytes, a valid PDF whose own content stream carries
// the string "Issue 1.55". This row exists because an earlier revision of this block recorded the
// OPPOSITE -- HeldRevisionIsCurrentPublicCopy -- on the strength of 1.55 being absent from the
// product brief. Absence from one index is not absence from the publisher.
//
// The standard that produced the wrong answer is nevertheless the right standard, and it is kept:
// a supersession is recorded only for a revision actually retrieved, never for one merely listed.
// 1.55 now meets it. Retiring this standing means ingesting 1.55 and re-deriving the rows that
// cite the datasheet -- section 2.10's active-channel facts are reported unchanged there, but
// "reported unchanged" is not a reading, and no row here is restated from it.
data ampere_altra_classic_rev_a1_datasheet_supersession_standing: HeldSpecificationSupersessionStanding = NewerRevisionPubliclyServed {
  newer_version_label: "1.55",
  newer_locator: "connect-admin.amperecomputing.com/api/secure-file-download/download-regular/?documentId=h51y6okvdig3i4u3lst6n120&file=Altra_Rev_A1_DS_v1_55_20250408_e814f50701.pdf&type=technical-document",
  index_authority: ampere_altra_device_documentation_authority,
  held_version_label: "v1.50",
  detail: "retrieved 2026-09-02: HTTP 200, 6630790 bytes, PDF self-identifying as Issue 1.55 dated 2025-04-08, while the cited locator pins v1.50 dated 2024-01-30; the family product brief still links v1.50 and is a stale index",
}

// THE ALTRA MAX CITATION IS SUPERSEDED, AND THE RECORDED TARGET IS THE FURTHEST REVISION THIS
// REPOSITORY HAS ACTUALLY RETRIEVED. v1.25 was fetched on 2026-09-02 (HTTP 200, ~6.6 MB) from the
// product brief. A v1.30 dated 2025-04-08 is REPORTED on the device-documentation surface, and if
// that report is right then ingesting v1.25 will not retire this debt. It is deliberately not
// named as the newer revision here: that surface is script-rendered, no v1.30 locator has been
// retrieved, and citing a document nobody has held is the precise error this block already made
// once about Issue 1.55 in the other direction. The debt is therefore recorded as AT LEAST v1.25.
data ampere_altra_max_rev_a1_datasheet_supersession_standing: HeldSpecificationSupersessionStanding = NewerRevisionPubliclyServed {
  newer_version_label: "v1.25",
  newer_locator: "amperecomputing.com/assets/Altra_Max_Rev_A1_DS_v1_25_20240130_73cfcc518a_4705c00046.pdf",
  index_authority: ampere_altra_device_documentation_authority,
  held_version_label: "v1.15",
  detail: "retrieved 2026-09-02: Altra Max Rev A1 DS v1.25 dated 2024-01-30 resolves HTTP 200 while the cited locator pins v1.15 dated 2023-08-09; a v1.30 dated 2025-04-08 is reported on the device-documentation surface but no locator for it has been retrieved, so the recorded target is the furthest revision actually held and this debt may extend past v1.25",
}

